WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.327
The most direct way to create a NumPy array is to start with a familiar Python sequence and

00:00:05.327 --> 00:00:07.872
pass it to N P dot array.

00:00:07.872 --> 00:00:12.032
The standard import is import NumPy as N P.

00:00:12.032 --> 00:00:18.772
The alias N P is used throughout NumPy documentation and in most professional Python code,

00:00:18.772 --> 00:00:21.447
so I recommend following that convention.

00:00:21.447 --> 00:00:25.492
In this example, we create an array of five scores.

00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:28.092
Click Run. The first output displays the numeric array.

00:00:28.092 --> 00:00:33.822
The second output confirms that the object is a NumPy ndarray rather than a regular Python list.

00:00:33.822 --> 00:00:39.867
Then we call the mean method directly on the array.

00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:42.622
NumPy calculates the average across all five scores.

00:00:42.622 --> 00:00:48.132
Notice how the array carries useful numerical behavior with it.

00:00:48.132 --> 00:00:52.667
We do not need to manually add every score and divide by the count.

00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:56.142
Try changing one of the scores and run the code again.

00:00:56.142 --> 00:00:58.467
The average updates immediately.

00:00:58.467 --> 00:01:04.532
This small example introduces the basic pattern we will use repeatedly: create an array,

00:01:04.532 --> 00:01:07.804
then apply array operations to the entire collection.
